<Project>

  <!--
    .NET Framework compared strings with Windows NLS. .NET 5 and later use ICU instead, and the two
    disagree about characters that "expand": under NLS the German sharp s compares equal to "ss",
    under ICU it does not.

    That is not a cosmetic difference here. ObjectByIdCache deliberately builds its dictionary
    comparer from LCID 1033 with IgnoreCase/IgnoreKanaType/IgnoreWidth in order to mimic the
    database's Latin1_General_CI_AS collation, so that an in-memory cache hit and a SQL unique index
    agree about what is the same name. SQL Server treats the sharp s as "ss"; ICU does not; so on
    ICU the cache misses, the importer inserts a second row, and SQL rejects it with a duplicate-key
    violation (2601). These caches carry every job title, department, office location, company and
    country in the tenant, and the working assumption for this product is a ~200,000-user tenant.

    How bad that is depends on the caller. DBLookupCache.GetOrCreateNewResource catches 2601/2627 and
    reloads - but only around its own SaveChangesAsync, which it performs solely when called with
    commitChangeOnSaveNew: true. The default overload passes false, and the high-volume path
    (UserBatchProcessor's pre-warm) uses that default and saves later, outside the handler. There the
    collision is not a recoverable round-trip: it fails the whole batch save.

    So this branch, whose purpose is to behave exactly like the .NET Framework build it mirrors, asks
    the runtime for the old behaviour. ObjectByIdCacheCollationTests is the focused proof and
    DataUtilsTests.CacheEncodingTest is the end-to-end one; both fail if this is removed.

    Caveat worth knowing before anyone moves a workload to Linux: this switch is Windows-only. On
    Linux the runtime always uses ICU and silently ignores it, so the caches would revert to the
    stricter comparison. Both web jobs - the cache-consuming workloads - target plain net10.0 and so
    would run there happily; what keeps this safe today is that the installer deploys them to a
    WINDOWS App Service plan (AppServicePlanTask uses Kind = "app"), not the target framework. The
    test project targets net10.0-windows, so the guard tests would not travel with such a move
    either. Treat Linux as an open decision, not a supported configuration.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.Globalization.UseNls" Value="true" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Linux build switch, for the Azure Container Apps demo (deploy/container-apps-demo) only.

    The projects that touch the installer engine target net10.0-windows because
    App.ControlPanel.Engine uses Windows Forms, and that framework reference flows to everything that
    references it - the web app included - so their runtimeconfig demands Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App,
    which does not exist on Linux. Building with -p:AnalyticsTargetOS=linux retargets those projects
    to plain net10.0 without Windows Forms, so the web app and the demo data generator can run in a
    Linux container. Any code that genuinely needs Windows Forms then fails to COMPILE rather than
    failing at runtime in a container.

    The default is unchanged: every normal build, CI build and installer build is net10.0-windows.
    The caveat above still applies - a Linux build ignores UseNls - which is why this switch is used
    for the demo web app and generator, and not for the importers.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalyticsTargetOS Condition="'$(AnalyticsTargetOS)' == ''">windows</AnalyticsTargetOS>
    <AnalyticsWindowsTargetFramework Condition="'$(AnalyticsTargetOS)' == 'linux'">net10.0</AnalyticsWindowsTargetFramework>
    <AnalyticsWindowsTargetFramework Condition="'$(AnalyticsTargetOS)' != 'linux'">net10.0-windows</AnalyticsWindowsTargetFramework>
    <AnalyticsUseWindowsForms Condition="'$(AnalyticsTargetOS)' == 'linux'">false</AnalyticsUseWindowsForms>
    <AnalyticsUseWindowsForms Condition="'$(AnalyticsTargetOS)' != 'linux'">true</AnalyticsUseWindowsForms>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Developer-local overrides. This file used to be gitignored so that a developer could drop their
    own settings in; now that the repository ships one, that hook moves here and the ignore rule
    follows it. Optional, and absent from a clean checkout.
  -->
  <Import Project="Directory.Build.local.props" Condition="Exists('Directory.Build.local.props')" />

</Project>
